        "author": "Source-reviewed explanations and ChatGPT proof roles",
        "prompt_version": 4
      }
    },
    "lean:Rat.isSquare_natCast_iff": {
      "title": "Preservation of squarehood",
      "summary": "Viewing a natural n as a rational allows more candidate roots, but no new square witnesses for n. If n = (a/b)² with a/b reduced and b > 0, the fraction a²/b² is still reduced. Since n has denominator 1, b = 1, and |a| is a natural square root.",
      "areas": [
        "Number theory"
      ],
      "compact_latex": "\\operatorname{IsSquare}_{\\mathbb Q}(n)\\iff\\operatorname{IsSquare}_{\\mathbb N}(n)",
      "concepts": [
        "coercion"
      ],
      "why_here": "This equivalence lets the natural-number criterion express squarehood entirely in the naturals.",
      "support_edges": [
        6
      ],
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "supplied_response",
        "file": "argument.response.json",
        "author": "Source-reviewed explanations and ChatGPT proof roles",
        "prompt_version": 4
      }
    },
    "lean:irrational_sqrt_natCast_iff": {
      "title": "Natural square criterion",
      "summary": "Apply the nonnegative rational criterion to n, viewed as a rational number. The squarehood transfer turns the condition about rational squares into a condition about natural squares. The natural-to-rational-to-real cast represents the same real n as the direct natural-to-real cast.",
      "areas": [
        "Number theory"
      ],
      "compact_latex": "\\sqrt n\\notin\\mathbb Q\\iff\\neg\\operatorname{IsSquare}_{\\mathbb N}(n)",
      "why_here": "The transfer step establishes the criterion, and the prime-root step uses its direction from nonsquare to irrational.",
      "support_edges": [
        5,
